daemon: add ar8030-lifecycled, a lifecycle supervisor for the chip

Tonight's investigation into flaky post-power-cycle reconnects found
the pile of independent shell-script watchdogs (autoreconnect,
device_watchdog, apply_link_tuning, the LED poller, the bind-button
poller) was fixing symptoms without addressing what actually owns the
AR8030 chip's lifecycle: init, pairing, reconnect-on-drop, and
notifying other applications of state changes. This adds that as one
dedicated process, ar8030-lifecycled, modeled on this project's own
prior successful scoped rewrite of the SDIO kernel driver
(ar8030-transport/kmod/artosyn_drv.c) -- absorb one well-understood
piece of vendor behavior into clean-room code, leave everything else
(the daemon's RPC serving, the ~90-entry ioctl surface, the socket
data-path) untouched.

Deliberately a separate process from ar8030d, not a thread inside it:
confirmed live that running this same connect/subscribe/poll logic as
a pthread inside the daemon process itself wedged the daemon (a
standalone test process doing the identical sequence ran clean; the
same code as an in-process thread produced 100+ stuck threads within a
minute). A genuinely separate process talking to ar8030d over loopback
-- exactly like ar8030-linkctl/ar8030-pair/ar8030-status already do --
does not share that risk.

What it does today: pulses the chip's hardware reset line at startup
(devmem or gpio-sysfs backend, runtime-selected by CLI flag), connects
to ar8030d over loopback with its own bounded-timeout event-subscribe
(BB_EVENT_LINK_STATE, BB_EVENT_PAIR_RESULT) plus a permanent
BB_GET_STATUS fallback poll as a safety net, triggers this project's
own pairing dispatch (ported from dev_helper/bb_pair/txg_bb_pair.cpp)
only if this unit has never been paired before, and follows real link
state transitions reported by the daemon. Confirmed live end-to-end
against a real ground-side peer: connect, subscribe, auto-pair, and
track live link-state transitions through to CONNECTED, with a
completely flat thread count sustained over a 5+ minute observation
window including a real SDIO fault-recovery cycle.

What it does not do yet, deliberately deferred rather than rushed:
tuning persistence (bandwidth/channel surviving a reconnect) and hook-
script dispatch on state transitions (so other tools -- LED control,
ifup/ifdown, starting/stopping the transport binary -- can react). The
board LED and any tuning-persistence behavior currently live in the
shell scripts this is meant to eventually replace; this patch does not
touch those scripts. The exponential backoff in its own reconnect loop
(2s, 4s, 8s, ..., capped at 60s) is deliberate, not just responsiveness
tuning: a flat, fast retry loop was confirmed live to sustain enough
loopback connection churn against ar8030d during an extended SDIO
outage to eventually wedge the daemon's own connection handling --
exactly the scenario a link-loss recovery tool must not make worse.

New files only; the existing daemon/rpc/socket code is untouched
except for the one line in dev_helper/CMakeLists.txt building this new
directory.

